WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
FedRAMP exists because federal cloud adoption created a practical challenge.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.100
Many agencies needed cloud services,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.060
but each agency also needed confidence that those services met appropriate security

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:10.985
expectations.

00:00:10.985 --> 00:00:14.900
If every agency performed a completely separate review of the same cloud service,

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.300
cloud adoption could become slower, more expensive, and less consistent.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.900
Providers would answer similar questions repeatedly,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.400
and agencies might receive evidence in different formats.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.560
FedRAMP helps reduce duplicated effort by standardizing cloud security assessment and

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.100
authorization activities.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.100
It promotes a common baseline for documentation, controls, assessment evidence,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.510
and continuous monitoring.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.085
Standardization supports risk reduction because it makes expectations clearer.

00:00:38.085 --> 00:00:41.100
Cloud providers can design programs around known requirements,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.700
assessors can evaluate against a recognized structure,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.300
and agencies can review evidence more consistently.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.100
FedRAMP also supports reuse.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.600
Security packages and assessment results can be reused across agencies,

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.715
while each agency remains responsible for its own risk decisions and mission context.

00:00:55.715 --> 00:00:59.800
The program does not remove the need for cybersecurity programs, agency judgment,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.650
or ongoing security operations.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:05.520
It gives organizations a common way to evaluate cloud security risk and maintain

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.800
visibility over time.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:07.520
In practice,

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.645
FedRAMP helps federal agencies adopt cloud technologies while preserving a disciplined

00:01:11.645 --> 00:01:12.800
approach to governance,

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.000
assessment, authorization, and monitoring.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.400
This standardization also helps cloud providers plan their programs.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.990
Instead of responding to completely different expectations for every customer,

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.900
providers can build security and evidence processes around a recognized federal model.
